thinking_content: "**Planning actions and insights**\n\nI need to produce some actions and maybe even code, while planning experiments too. Right now, I'm working as Ariadne II and have submitted an evaluation. My submission is queued, which gives me time to explore Ariadne I's private memory for useful insights.\n\nI'll check the file defaults.py to ensure everything's set up correctly. If there are any errors in my submission, the auto-debugger will handle those. I can also look into the lineage capsules for relevant information and plan future experiments involving embeddings and memory.**Evaluating JAX memory usage**\n\nI'm looking into how JAX on GPU stores parameters, estimating around 2.3 MB for storage (assuming float32 sizes), alongside the dense layers. Memory might be higher due to batch normalization since I'm using layer normalization with specific parameters. For the features, the last dimension is 12, which means the layer normalization will have gamma and beta of that size.\
  \ \n\nAlso, the input to the dense layer has a dimension of 12, and the weight shape looks good. Ah, I’ve noticed that the dense layer applies to each neuron individually, sharing weights across neurons. That's a relief!**Assessing compute and memory impacts**\n\nThis approach gives me computing for B*N nodes, where B can go up to 8 and N is around 71k, leading to about 573k rows. So, that's around 1.1 billion multiply-add operations, which seems heavy, though it might be manageable on a GPU. \n\nThe baseline models operate per-neuron MLP and likely vectorize across the N dimension, fitting expected compute. However, I'm concerned about memory usage: flattening features from (B,N,12) to (B*N,12) might lead to high activation memory, around 27 MB, and for the next layer, it stretches to about 367 MB, which is too large!**Identifying memory issues**\n\nI realize there's a problem; the baseline might be using smaller hidden dimensions or a per-neuron MLP across 32 outputs. They didn't flatten\
  \ to a 160 dimension across B*N, but they still have a similar shape. So, when calculating memory for each neuron with a hidden size of 160 multiplied by B*N, that creates a huge activation memory footprint.\n\nThe baseline seems to work with H=160 using layer normalization and maybe dropout. I wonder how they managed memory. They could have utilized approximate microbatches and JIT compilation. Still, activation memory for backprop needs to be stored. Also, I think Ray training uses gradient checkpointing, but I'm not entirely sure. Interestingly, the baseline runs within 24GB of memory.**Evaluating memory and planning actions**\n\nI find that 91.8 million floats equal about 367 MB, which isn’t too big. It fits within the 24GB limit, and other arrays, like y and gradients, will add some memory too. I noticed broadcasting parameters might increase the size to only 18.4 MB, which is manageable.\n\nHowever, the computation across all neurons could be heavy. The baseline architecture used\
  \ Fourier features to represent time dimensions, while our simpler setup leverages raw timesteps and produces 32 outputs. As I wait for evaluations, I plan to explore private memory for insights and set a to-do list in YAML format.   \n\nI’ll check the baseline code, review the defaults, and organize tasks in private memory while keeping an eye on submission evaluations for further insights.**Planning actions and evaluations**\n\nAlright, I see that those evaluations with scores are done, so it’s time to review them. I want to focus on evaluation 450, which seems to be the top one, and we'll check the code and logs. Since retrieving the code may be heavy, I’ll keep an eye on token usage but feel confident in our budget.\n\nI'll include multiple actions to maximize our time. The plan includes reviewing the baseline, checking storage defaults, and navigating private memory. I’ll also request help to understand the actions I can take there. Let’s proceed with those actions! \n\nTo summarize:\n\
  \n- `/execute_action{review 1}`\n- `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`\n- `/execute_action{review 450}`\n- `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n- `/execute_action{help private_memory}` \n\nWith this approach, I’m keeping things organized and efficient!"
