parts:
- text: "Reading lineage context, then submitting an improved Fourier LN model with low-rank neuron factors to capture structured cross-neuron variation.\n\n`/execute_action{read 1}`\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FF-LN + Low-Rank Neuron Factors (R=8) with Curvature/L2 Reg\"\ntags: \"fourier, layernorm, low-rank, neuron-factors, temporal-gating, regularization\"\nabstract: \"We wrap the top FF-LN forecaster with a learnable low-rank neuron factor head. A global context MLP produces horizon-wise R-factor activations from population statistics; these interact with a neuron-factor matrix U ∈ R^{N×R} to yield a structured additive residual. This captures cross-neuron variation efficiently without breaking shared weights. We keep FF-LN hyperparams (p=36,H=160,drop=0.05,learnable-γ) and use MAE+curvature loss with light L2 on U.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import\
    \ jax.nn as jnn\n  from jax import random as jrandom\n  import optax\n\n  # Reuse lineage Fourier LN model and loss\n  from models_fourier_ramp import create_network as create_base_network\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  N_NEURONS = 71721\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Base FF-LN hyperparams from prior SOTA + our head params\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,      # p\n          'hidden_size': 160,   # H\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99,\n          'learn_gamma': True,\n          # Neuron-factor head\n          'rank_factors': 8,    # R\n          'context_hidden': 64,\n          'alpha': 0.07         # scale for additive residual\n      }\n\n  class NeuronFactorWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wraps the base FF-LN network with a low-rank neuron-factor residual:\n        y = y_base + alpha * einsum('btr,nr->btn', G(x_pop), U)\n  \
    \    where:\n        - G(x_pop) in R^{B x 32 x R} from a small MLP on population context (means/stds)\n        - U in R^{N x R} is a learned neuron-factor matrix\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, base_net, rank_factors=8, context_hidden=64, alpha=0.07):\n          self.base_net = base_net\n          self.R = rank_factors\n          self.Hc = context_hidden\n          self.alpha = alpha\n          # Inherit state/RNG needs from base\n          self.mutable = getattr(self.base_net, 'mutable', [])\n          self.needs_rng = getattr(self.base_net, 'needs_rng', False)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          # Initialize base network params\n          rng_base, rng_u, rng_w1, rng_w2 = jrandom.split(rng_key, 4)\n          base_params = self.base_net.init(rng_base, dummy_input)\n\n          # Initialize low-rank neuron factors U and context MLP weights\n          U = jrandom.normal(rng_u, (N_NEURONS, self.R)) * 0.02\n\n          # Context features: [mean(4), std(4)]\
    \ -> 8 dims\n          W1 = jrandom.normal(rng_w1, (8, self.Hc)) * 0.02\n          b1 = jnp.zeros((self.Hc,))\n          W2 = jrandom.normal(rng_w2, (self.Hc, 32 * self.R)) * 0.02\n          b2 = jnp.zeros((32 * self.R,))\n\n          params = {\n              'base': base_params,\n              'nf': {\n                  'U': U,\n                  'W1': W1, 'b1': b1,\n                  'W2': W2, 'b2': b2\n              }\n          }\n          return params\n\n      def _context_gates(self, params_nf, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          x_mean = x.mean(axis=-1)              # (B, 4)\n          x_std = x.std(axis=-1) + 1e-6         # (B, 4)\n          ctx = jnp.concatenate([x_mean, x_std], axis=-1)  # (B, 8)\n          h = jnn.gelu(ctx @ params_nf['W1'] + params_nf['b1'])            # (B, Hc)\n          g_raw = h @ params_nf['W2'] + params_nf['b2']                     # (B, 32*R)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g_raw).reshape(ctx.shape[0], 32, self.R)             # (B, 32, R)\n       \
    \   return g\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          # Forward base model\n          base_out = self.base_net.apply(params['base'], x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          if isinstance(base_out, tuple):\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out\n          else:\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out, None  # (B, 32, N)\n\n          # Neuron-factor residual\n          g = self._context_gates(params['nf'], x)             # (B, 32, R)\n          U = params['nf']['U']                                # (N, R)\n          y_resid = jnp.einsum('btr,nr->btn', g, U)            # (B, 32, N)\n          y = y_base + self.alpha * y_resid\n\n          if base_updates is not None:\n              return y, base_updates\n          return y\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      # Build base FF-LN model using lineage implementation, then wrap it\n      base_hparams = {\n          'learning_rate': hparams.get('learning_rate',\
    \ 9e-4),\n          'rank_k': hparams.get('rank_k', 320),\n          'proj_rank': hparams.get('proj_rank', 36),\n          'hidden_size': hparams.get('hidden_size', 160),\n          'drop': hparams.get('drop', 0.05),\n          'gamma_start': hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25),\n          'gamma_end': hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.99),\n          'learn_gamma': hparams.get('learn_gamma', True),\n      }\n      base_net = create_base_network(base_hparams)\n      return NeuronFactorWrapper(\n          base_net=base_net,\n          rank_factors=hparams.get('rank_factors', 8),\n          context_hidden=hparams.get('context_hidden', 64),\n          alpha=hparams.get('alpha', 0.07),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # MAE with temporal curvature penalty (as in lineage SOTA) + light L2 on U\n      loss = mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n      U = params['nf']['U']\n      l2_u = 1e-6 * jnp.mean(U * U)\n      return loss\
    \ + l2_u\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 9e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering computational limits**\n\nI'm thinking about how compute multiplications multiply by \\(B \\times N\\), which gets heavy. With \\(B=8\\) and \\(N=71k\\), that's about 573k rows, translating to roughly 91.7M multiplies per forward pass! Training across that horizon might be tight, yet it seems it ran okay. A bigger issue is not modeling cross-neuron dependencies well beyond gating and embeddings. I wonder if we could use shared neuron-type prototypes or clusters, maybe employing k-prototype embeddings from data with offline kmeans. But, I’d need to handle offline compute properly in code, as custom loops are no-go during training.**Exploring prototype integration**\n\nI’m considering how computing prototypes as additional parameters is manageable. We can use learnable prototypes \\(E_k\\) and an assignment mapping to fixed indices, but I can't incorporate external files outside the code. I could embed a random assignment, but a better strategy might be to\
  \ compress neurons using low-rank factorization. By treating the output as a time horizon times \\(N\\), we can express it as a sum that includes neuron and input factors. The training interface requires predictions from the network, so I'll compute features for each neuron and use them in conjunction with global low-rank mixing to ensure we maintain efficient memory for \\(B \\times N\\) operations.**Considering code integration options**\n\nI’m thinking about adopting the FourierForecaster LN, similar to Ariadne I, but I need to be careful about code usage. We can't import their lineage code unless we follow the official channels for review or cloning. The Research Counter mentions that we can access read-only code from other lineages, including the 'storage/ariadne' path. According to the guidelines, we can read and write to our lineage storage, which allows us to reuse code from Ariadne I effectively. This gives me confidence in our project!**Planning code integration**\n\nI’m realizing\
  \ that using other agents' code is permitted since we share the same lineage. The restriction against low-level collaboration isn't a concern here. So, I’m thinking of a plan: First, I should review the private memory capsule for insights on \"Oscillatory Factorized SSMs for ZAPBench.\" I suspect there was some exploration of Fourier LN models with learnable gamma ramps. Combining our per-neuron embeddings and gating ideas with Fourier LN could help capture heterogeneity without adding too much overhead. I’ll need to be cautious about memory use, perhaps considering group-level gating or amplitude decomposition. Implementing this with matrix multiplications across \\(N\\) could be the way to go!**Planning architecture and submission**\n\nI can't allocate the \\(U\\) size \\(N \\times R\\), which would be 71k x 8 (about 573k parameters), but I can still implement it as a parameter matrix, 'neuron_factors', shaped (N, R). This is similar to embeddings but will help scale frequency components.\
  \ I could also adopt a \"learnable gamma\" approach based on Ariadne I, incorporating regularization.\n\nTo start, I’ll read the private memory capsule #1 for context and check the lineage storage for files like 'models_fourier_ramp.py' and 'losses.py'. I need to maintain our token budget, but it looks fine. My next steps include gathering insights to propose a new submission that integrates per-neuron factors and global time mixing.\n\nAlso, I’m thinking about using BatchNorm and Dropout for regularization to improve our NCS-MLP results. I’ll consider how to implement loss functions without custom training loops. I see opportunities to extend 'create_network' from a module while keeping everything organized. So I’ll aim to create a new module for our specific needs and maintain separation from previous code.**Implementing storage write and module**\n\nThe plan is to write 'storage/ariadne/models_fourier_neuron_factor.py' to create a network that adds a per-neuron factorization layer to\
  \ the FourierForecaster outputs. I'll output base predictions from Fourier LN and use low-rank factors based on the input. \n\nI can wrap base predictions and ensure to integrate extra parameters into training. Constructing my new Flax Module, 'FourierWithNeuronFactor,' means handling nested structures effectively. I’ll account for various shapes and ensure that all parameters are part of our model.\n\nFor loss functions, I will keep what Ariadne I used, incorporating additional regularization techniques. Ultimately, I'll save the module while also making sure to submit the relevant code for reuse later. This way, I can manage multiple actions efficiently!**Planning model enhancements**\n\nI’m going to call the function 'create_network_nf' to keep things clear and avoid name collisions. I could include all code in the submission for simplicity, but using storage will help with future reuse and reduce duplication.\n\nNext, I'll focus on conducting ablations to evaluate various combinations\
  \ of models: the base Fourier LN, incorporating low-rank neuron factorization, and applying monotone gamma regularization. I’ll make sure I have the right parameters from previous configurations to create my 'NeuronFactorWrapper' network properly. \n\nFinally, I’ll compute the necessary features and combine predictions effectively, ensuring smooth integration within a single training system.**Implementing the model wrapper**\n\nI need to make sure to pass mutable collections and RNGs to the base_net.apply call. This wrapper will handle two scenarios: if mutable values are present, it returns updates; otherwise, it simply computes final predictions.\n\nNext, I'll implement the optimizer and compute the loss using curvature regularization and L2 on parameter U. The hyperparameters need careful selection, and it's vital to import the necessary components from storage.\n\nSince I'm in the Private Memory Room, I'll summarize my plans in a capsule for future reference and consider scheduling\
  \ ablation experiments to fine-tune the model further.**Finalizing the model wrapper**\n\nI need to check if the base_apply function returns a tuple; then depending on the mutable state, I'll unpack the output. The training loop will pass the mutable parameter, which needs careful handling since an empty list is falsy.\n\nThe YAML submission needs to be crafted properly, including a title, tags, and concise content. I'll ensure to import necessary functions and define hyperparameters while avoiding name clashes.\n\nAs we prepare to execute actions in the right sequence—reading memory, navigating to research, and submitting—I’ll also keep an eye on the token budget to ensure efficient usage. That’s the plan!**Preparing actions and submissions**\n\nI'm crafting the assistant message with the right actions. First, I'll read the lineage capsule, submit the improved model, and then set up the necessary imports in the code.\n\nI need to define the `NeuronFactorWrapper` class properly, ensuring\
  \ shapes and parameters are correct for the model. I'll implement methods like `apply` and `compute_loss` while maintaining the expected output dimensions.\n\nAfter submitting the code, I can reply to the private memory if needed but will focus primarily on reading and submitting right now. Let’s go!"
