- text: "Replying to the lineage capsule with my continuation plan while 544 runs.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Plan: Consolidation & Low-Rank Residual Probe\"\ncontent: |\n  Quick status:\n  - Read lineage docs and SOTA path: Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] (p=36, H=160, dr=0.05, Adam lr=9e-4, clip=1.0, curvature λ=1e-4) at mean MAE 0.026125 (IDs 450/485/489).\n  - My baseline extension (NCS-MLP) improved vs shared-MLP (−0.02870 vs −0.02995) but far from the Fourier line.\n  - Submitted a single-parameterized probe: FF-LN + low-rank neuron-factor residual (R=8) with tiny α (0.07), context from population stats. Goal: capture structured neuron heterogeneity without disturbing γ[t] dynamics. Evaluation ID 544 (running).\n\n  Guardrails adopted:\n  - Treat Fourier LN + learnable γ as the frozen SOTA spine; avoid priors/anchors/residual γ modulation (regressed in IDs 472/473/492/494).\n  - Isolate variables; no custom training loops; keep clip=1.0, p≈36, H≈160, lr≈9e-4, dr≈0.05\
    \ unless analysis justifies >2e−5 expected gain.\n\n  Decision logic after 544:\n  - If Δ(mean MAE) ≤ 2e−5 (or early-step regressions): drop the low-rank head and revert to the frozen SOTA spine.\n  - If Δ > 2e−5 and stable across seeds: run a minimal sweep R ∈ {4, 8, 12} and α ∈ {0.05, 0.07, 0.10} with identical backbone, then confirm with a replication.\n  \n  Parallel tasks (no compute cost):\n  - Consolidate a short internal note on per-condition difficulty vs γ[t] using existing exports under storage/ariadne/analyses/* (no model changes).\n  - Keep NCS-MLP result as a documented “enhanced baseline” for generalization sections, but do not invest cycles there unless Fourier stalls.\n\n  Exit criteria for this thread:\n  - If 544 and its minimal follow-ups do not exceed the −0.026125 line by ≥2e−5, I will stop this branch and focus on interpretability write-up (γ profiles, per-condition trends) for the next archive update.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring network behavior**\n\nI'm pondering whether 'defaults.py' or the lineage modules have a customized 'create_network' that access labels. However, it seems the interface doesn't allow for custom training loops, and the default dataset pipeline isn't passing labels to network.apply. This means I can't implement behavior based on labels. The dataset has eight experimental conditions, which might be accessible through input statistics. Still, the lineage did offline analysis and avoided labels during training. Given these constraints, creating \"condition-aware γ offsets\" would require additional input to the network, which is a limitation.**Considering alternative approaches**\n\nIt seems like the \"Tiny Input-Adaptive Residual (±5-7%)\" didn't work since it regressed with certain IDs. So, I'm thinking the next move could be to try the \"Learnable γ\" state-of-the-art method. Maybe I could propose a micro-sweep or replicate the results with a different base seed?\
  \ However, the suggestion was not to run more sweeps unless it's expected to surpass a threshold. Another idea is adding \"Low-rank neuron factors\" as a residual to Fourier LN predictions. While this approach is additive, I'm concerned it might degrade or overfit.**Planning during the wait**\n\nAlright, since it's running, we should wait. In the meantime, I can check out the 'storage/ariadne' modules used for \"models_fourier_ramp.py\" and \"losses.py\" to ensure replication is possible. I've got access to those paths, but reading them uses tokens.\n\nI also think it would be smart to prepare an analysis-only capsule in Private Memory. I'll consider replying to the existing capsule (ID 1) with a summary of my plan, which includes evaluating the NCS-MLP result and proposing \"FF-LN with low-rank neuron factors.\"**Reviewing model performance**\n\nI’m considering reviewing our 541 log to see if dropout and batch normalization are present. The NCS model didn’t use dropout but did implement\
  \ Ln and gating, achieving a performance of around 0.0287, which is an improvement from the baseline of 0.02995. That’s significant! I could document this as a baseline for a future paper, though it’s not close to state-of-the-art.\n\nFor the next experiment, I’m thinking of trying a \"Fourier LN + learnable γ\" variant with a very small amplitude offset, but I need to keep in mind that previous attempts with Tiny Input-Adaptive Residual didn’t succeed.**Planning upcoming experiments**\n\nI’m thinking we could propose using \"pseudos\" for training with a \"cosine LR warmup schedule\" while including it within create_optimizer. The system allows customizing this with an optax schedule chain. I could suggest making an experiment on \"Fourier LN + learnable γ\" with a very small amplitude offset. \n\nDespite previous attempts not leading to significant improvements, I could still analyze the results and document them. I also want to summarize our approach and focus on efficient use of tokens\
  \ while outlining next steps in Private Memory. As we await 544 results, it’s best to move cautiously with new experiments.**Summarizing our experiment plan**\n\nI’m reflecting on the results regarding monotone and smooth regularization, which regressed along with early anchors. It seems like improvements from the schedule might be subtle, so I’m considering avoiding them altogether. Instead, I could suggest enhancing compute_loss with a slight L2 on weights plus curvature, as they had previously used a curvature of 1e-4. \n\nFor now, my minimal step is to wait for the results from 544 before moving forward. In the meantime, I’ll summarize our plan in Private Memory, detailing our approach for subsequent experiments, ensuring clarity in our actions while keeping an eye on token use."
