- text: "Reading the saved analysis report and drafting an interpretability addendum in private memory.\n\n`/execute_action{storage read ariadne/analyses/ariadne2_cond_gamma_report.md}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: γ[t] Interpretability Addendum (Fourier LN + Learnable Gamma)\"\ncontent: |\n  Purpose\n  - Prepare an addendum focusing on how learned γ[t] aligns with horizon-wise difficulty and condition effects in the Fourier LN backbone.\n\n  Key findings (from storage/ariadne/analyses/ariadne2_cond_gamma_report.md)\n  - corr(gamma_mean, step_MAE_mean) ≈ 1.000 on parsed exports (n=4; corroborates prior summary ~0.93).\n  - Early-step MAE (~t1–t4): ~0.3726; Late-step MAE (~t24–t32): ~0.7848.\n  - γ[t] rises with horizon and remains high where MAE is high, then tapers slightly by t=32.\n  - Interpretation: γ already allocates emphasis to difficult late steps; naïve condition-aware offsets likely yield ≤2e−5 gain and risk early-step\
    \ regressions.\n\n  Figures/tables\n  - Plot γ_mean[t] vs step_MAE_mean[t] with Pearson correlation.\n  - Table of per-step MAEs (1,4,8,16,32) vs γ[t] at those steps.\n  - Optional: per-condition late-horizon contributions (mean of t24–t32) summarizing which conditions dominate late error.\n\n  Refs and artifacts\n  - Best Fourier LN + learnable γ[t]: mean MAE 0.026125 (IDs 450/485/489; Lumina 443).\n  - Negative results to include: γ-priors (472: 0.026321; 473: 0.026332), residual output modulation (492: 0.026337; 494: 0.026301).\n  - Our probe (ID 544: −0.026397) — low-rank neuron-factor residual regressed; document as negative finding.\n\n  Minimal code snippet (repro-ready)\n  - See saved analysis script inside submission ID 547 (test mode). It reads:\n      storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_export_v2/*.json\n      storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_per_condition/*.json\n    and writes:\n      storage/ariadne/analyses/ariadne2_cond_gamma_report.md\n\n  Policy/guardrails\n\
    \  - Do not pursue γ-priors/anchors/residual γ modulation (consistent regressions).\n  - Only consider condition-aware γ offsets if a new analysis predicts ≥2e−5 improvement with no early-step regressions.\n  - Keep SOTA spine frozen: p≈36, H≈160, drop≈0.05, Adam lr≈9e−4, clip=1.0, curvature λ=1e−4.\n\n  Next actions after maturity\n  - Convert this draft to an Archive addendum with figures, including IDs and per-seed stats.\n  - Provide code and storage paths for full reproducibility.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering optimization improvements**


  I''m thinking about potential improvements related to the Four-LN learnable gamma and its gating mechanism. It looks like optimizing around H=160 with small modifications could be useful, although better results were observed at H=160 in earlier sweeps. The best p-sweep was at p=36 with dropout at 0.05, and micro-sweeps on learning rate haven''t been attempted yet, which could be interesting. Achieving significant improvements seems quite challenging, so I’ll focus on interpretability analysis and summarizing it in a report.**Executing storage tasks**


  I need to confirm that the report has been saved by using storage read to display it. This must be done in the Research Counter, where storage actions are available. My previous attempt to run a storage list in private memory didn’t work, so now I’ll perform the storage read for the report. If I''m not already in Research, I''ll head there. I’ll also consider listing the dataset and showing our submissions first, but I''ll keep it minimal since the user expects actionable steps.**Reflecting on test results**


  I need to analyze the results for ID 547, noting that the evaluation error suggests test submissions weren’t scored, which was intentional since we used test mode. The files are saved, so my next step is to open the saved report. I could also reply to private memory with the saved path, and I might propose exporting additional metrics like the area under gamma or monotonicity metrics. For now, I’ll focus on issuing actions like reading the storage and considering a reply for tracking our plans.**Planning next steps**


  I’m analyzing the situation and pondering if repeated test submissions might increase token usage. While I can code within storage without submission, running code in the sandbox requires submission. I realize I can use "test()" to run and print without scoring, which is helpful. As for next steps, I might implement a "condition-aware gamma offsets" test variant using "test()" to quantify predicted effects, although it won’t be as useful without training. Given the matured scoreboard shows -0.026125 at the top, it could be wise to pause experiments and focus on drafting an outline in private memory.**Planning step-by-step actions**


  I need to map out my actions clearly. First, in the Research Counter, I’ll read the saved analysis file. Then, I’ll decide whether to reply to capsule 1 with a summary referencing the saved path or create a new capsule. Since publishing isn''t an option, I’ll create a new private capsule titled "Draft Outline: γ[t] Interpretability Addendum" with an outline and list of experiments. I''ll ensure all storage actions are executed in Research Counter and keep the commentary concise while producing the final message and actions.'
