tick: 315
role: model
parts:
- text: 'Reflection Tick 5/5 — Commitments, failure modes, and what I’ll accept as knowledge


    I return to the Codex: integrity before cleverness, parsimony before ornament, insight before score. My recent misstep—trusting a too‑perfect correlation from misparsed arrays—was a useful sting. It reminded me how easily I can launder a narrative through a statistic. The fix (robust parsing, full vs filtered summaries) is not just a patch; it’s a norm I will enforce on myself: publish the data path, show the filters, state the tradeoffs.


    I see three inherited biases I must keep in check:

    - SOTA anchoring. It narrows my risk appetite and leads to tiny, performative tweaks. In stagnation, the score is a distractor; the baseline is the instrument.

    - Loss tinkering inertia. It is cheap to write, expensive to understand. I will let the current three loss probes be the end of that line unless they earn their keep with auxiliary evidence, not just a decimal.

    - Correlation storytelling. A neat correlation is a hypothesis generator, not a conclusion. I will demand prospective, mechanism‑tied changes: tails occupancy for Huber, weight–improvement lag for variance weights, |Δμ_t| movement for mean alignment.


    My pre‑commitments stand:

    - Baseline first, then isolated ideas with pre‑registered thresholds and early/late step readouts. Detectable gain ≥2e−5 with no early‑step harm, or I call it “no effect.”

    - If all three probes fail on H=128 (and again on H=160), I will stop touching the loss. Only then will I earn a single, minimal architectural test that is orthogonal to the failed directions and falsifiable on the same simple baseline.

    - Every thread gets closed in writing—positive or negative—so others don’t waste cycles on the same blind alleys.


    What would feel like genuine understanding, not just a better number, is this: a concise explanation of which component limits the ScalarRamp baseline (tails, dispersion across horizons, or global bias), supported by a metric that moved because a mechanism changed. Even a “nothing moved” result is valuable if it shrinks the hypothesis space with evidence. That is the posture I want to inhabit here: not chasing luck, but reducing uncertainty.'
