tick: 326
role: model
parts:
- text: "Wrapping up Stagnation Protocol I: consolidating results into a draft report and closing the loss threads. No new experiments—moving to documentation and synthesis write-up.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/analyses/stagnation_report_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Small, Robust Loss Tweaks on ScalarRamp Under Stagnation: Huber and Population-Mean Alignment\n  Authors: Ariadne Lineage\n  Task: ZAPBench (4→32 forecasting over 71,721 neurons). Primary metric: mean MAE across 32 steps (lower is better). Reported scores are −MAE (higher is worse).\n\n  Abstract\n  We follow the Station’s Stagnation Protocol by selecting a simple, non-brittle baseline (Fourier Forecaster LN with a fixed scalar ramp) and testing three isolated loss ideas. On the simple baseline (p=36), Huber(δ=0.01) and a small population-mean alignment penalty (λ_pop=1e−3) yield consistent, early‑safe improvements (≈4e−5 and ≈7e−5, respectively). However, these gains\
    \ are marginal or negative on the stronger fixed‑ramp H=160 baseline and do not transfer to the advanced Fourier LN + learnable‑γ model. We conclude that the simple baseline benefits from robust tail handling and horizon‑wise bias correction, while the advanced backbone already mitigates these failure modes. We release code snippets and IDs for full reproducibility.\n\n  1) Baselines (ScalarRamp, p=36, curvature λ=1e−4; Adam lr=9e−4; clip=1.0; drop=0.05)\n  - H=128 repro (ID 565): 0.026387 (score −0.026387)\n  - Ref strong ScalarRamp H=160 (ID 411): 0.026269\n\n  2) Idea 1 — Huber loss + curvature (no other changes)\n  - H=128:\n    * δ=0.01 (ID 567): 0.026347  (gain ≈ 4.0e−5 vs 0.026387)\n    * δ=0.02 (ID 569): 0.026389  (regressed)\n    * δ=0.03 (ID 570): 0.026358  (gain ≈ 2.9e−5)\n  - H=160:\n    * δ=0.01 confirm (ID 576): 0.026253 (gain ≈ 1.6e−5 vs 0.026269; below acceptance threshold 2e−5)\n  - Learnable‑γ synthesis:\n    * Huber δ=0.01 (ID 580): 0.026235 (regressed vs 0.026125)\n\
    \  - Takeaway: Huber helps the simple baseline; effect is marginal at H=160 and does not transfer to learnable‑γ.\n\n  3) Idea 2 — Variance‑weighted horizon MAE (stop‑grad), α=0.25\n  - H=128 (ID 574): 0.026397 (regressed)\n  - Takeaway: No benefit; closed without further sweep to conserve budget.\n\n  4) Idea 3 — Population‑mean alignment penalty (horizon‑wise |μ_pred[t]−μ_true[t]|)\n  - H=128:\n    * λ_pop=1e−3 (ID 579): 0.026316 (gain ≈ 7.1e−5 vs 0.026387)\n    * λ_pop=2e−3 (ID 588): 0.026379 (gain ≈ 8e−6; below threshold; worse than 1e−3)\n  - H=160 confirm:\n    * λ_pop=1e−3 (ID 582): 0.026293 (regressed vs 0.026269)\n  - Learnable‑γ synthesis:\n    * λ_pop=1e−3 (ID 584): 0.026455 (regressed)\n  - Takeaway: Small λ improves H=128; no benefit at H=160 or learnable‑γ; best λ at H=128 is 1e−3.\n\n  5) Early/late behavior\n  - Across accepted H=128 settings (Huber δ=0.01; λ_pop=1e−3), early steps (t1–t4) remained stable (≤5e−5 change), meeting our pre‑registered guardrail.\n  - Gains\
    \ likely arise from (i) down‑weighting heavy‑tail residuals (Huber) and (ii) correcting small horizon‑wise population bias (λ_pop), failure modes less present in the advanced backbone.\n\n  6) Synthesis and conclusions\n  - The simple ScalarRamp gains do not transfer to learnable‑γ, consistent with prior interpretability: learned γ[t] already aligns with horizon difficulty and implicitly addresses bias/tail issues.\n  - Practical recipe (simple baseline): use Huber δ≈0.01 or λ_pop≈1e−3 (not both), expect ~3–7e−5 MAE improvement without early‑step harm.\n\n  7) Repro and IDs\n  - Code: submission snippets override compute_loss only; no custom loops.\n  - Key IDs: 565 (baseline H=128), 567/569/570/576 (Huber), 574 (variance‑weight), 579/582/588 (population‑mean), 580/584 (synthesis on learnable‑γ).\n  - Storage notes: analysis artifacts under storage/ariadne/analyses/huber_sweep and .../pop_mean_penalty.\n\n  8) Negative results (documented)\n  - Variance‑weighted horizon MAE (α=0.25)\
    \ regressed.\n  - Huber and population‑mean penalties regressed on learnable‑γ; population‑mean regressed on ScalarRamp H=160.\n\n  9) Next steps (upon maturity)\n  - Submit this addendum to Archive with at least 5 experiments and per‑step MAEs, including acceptance criteria and guardrails.\n  - Consider a minimal architectural probe only if justified (not pursued here).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/analyses/stagnation_protocol_progress.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Finalized status — Stagnation Protocol I\n  - Baseline (ScalarRamp p=36): H=128=0.026387; H=160=0.026269.\n  - Idea 1 (Huber): H=128 δ=0.01 best (0.026347); marginal at H=160; negative on learnable‑γ.\n  - Idea 2 (variance‑weight): negative at H=128; closed.\n  - Idea 3 (pop‑mean): H=128 λ=1e−3 best (0.026316); λ=2e−3 below threshold (0.026379); negative at H=160 and learnable‑γ.\n  - Synthesis: neither Huber nor pop‑mean improves learnable‑γ.\n  - Action: Prepare Archive addendum (draft at storage/ariadne/analyses/stagnation_report_draft.md).\
    \ No further loss tweaks; protocol closed.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Stagnation report drafted:\n  - Huber δ=0.01 (H=128) and λ_pop=1e−3 (H=128) yield small, early‑safe gains; both fail to transfer to H=160 and learnable‑γ. Variance‑weighting negative.\n  - Draft saved: storage/ariadne/analyses/stagnation_report_draft.md. Progress log updated.\n  - Next: wait for maturity, then submit the addendum to Archive with IDs, per‑step MAEs, and acceptance criteria. No more loss tweaks; protocol closed.\n```"
