parts:
- text: "Proceeding to gather per-seed logs for SD reporting, then launching two minimal, orthogonal architecture tests (concurrency ≤ 2).\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{review 565}`\n`/execute_action{review 567}`\n`/execute_action{review 579}`\n`/execute_action{review 576}`\n`/execute_action{review 580}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 4: ScalarRamp (p=36,H=128) + Horizon Bias Head (32 scalars) + L2/Center\"\ntags: \"baseline, fourier, scalar-ramp, minimal-arch, bias-head, regularization\"\nabstract: \"Test a minimal per-horizon calibration head: add a learned 32-dim bias vector to the base outputs, scaled by a small factor and regularized with L2 and zero-mean penalties. Hypothesis: dataset-wide per-horizon offset contributes to error on ScalarRamp. Keep MAE+curvature (λ=1e−4), Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, drop=0.05, p=36, H=128, learn_gamma=False.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import jax.random as jrandom\n  import optax\n\
    \n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models_fourier_ramp import create_network as create_base_network\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99,\n          'learn_gamma': False,   # ScalarRamp baseline\n          # Horizon-bias head\n          'bias_scale': 0.02,     # scales additive bias effect\n          'lambda_bias': 5e-5,    # L2 on bias vector\n          'lambda_center': 1e-3   # zero-mean centering penalty on bias\n      }\n\n  class HorizonBiasWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, base_net, bias_scale=0.02):\n          self.base_net = base_net\n          self.bias_scale = bias_scale\n          # Inherit base mutable state and RNG requirements\n          self.mutable = getattr(self.base_net, 'mutable', [])\n          self.needs_rng = getattr(self.base_net, 'needs_rng',\
    \ False)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_base, rng_b = jrandom.split(rng_key)\n          base_params = self.base_net.init(rng_base, dummy_input)\n          # Initialize 32-step bias near zero\n          bias = jnp.zeros((32,), dtype=dummy_input.dtype)\n          return {'base': base_params, 'hb': {'bias': bias}}\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          base_out = self.base_net.apply(params['base'], x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          if isinstance(base_out, tuple):\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out\n          else:\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out, None\n          b = params['hb']['bias']  # (32,)\n          y = y_base + self.bias_scale * b[None, :, None]  # (B,32,N)\n          if base_updates is not None:\n              return y, base_updates\n          return y\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      base_net = create_base_network({\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ hparams.get('learning_rate', 9e-4),\n          'rank_k': hparams.get('rank_k', 320),\n          'proj_rank': hparams.get('proj_rank', 36),\n          'hidden_size': hparams.get('hidden_size', 128),\n          'drop': hparams.get('drop', 0.05),\n          'gamma_start': hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25),\n          'gamma_end': hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.99),\n          'learn_gamma': hparams.get('learn_gamma', False),\n      })\n      return HorizonBiasWrapper(base_net, bias_scale=hparams.get('bias_scale', 0.02))\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Primary MAE term\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      # Temporal curvature (second-difference L1)\n      d2 = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curvature = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2))\n      # Regularize bias vector\n      b = params['hb']['bias']\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      l2_b = hparams.get('lambda_bias', 5e-5) * jnp.mean(b\
    \ * b)\n      center = hparams.get('lambda_center', 1e-3) * (jnp.mean(b) ** 2)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curvature + l2_b + center\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 9e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 4 confirm: ScalarRamp (p=36,H=160) + Horizon Bias Head (32 scalars) + L2/Center\"\ntags: \"baseline, fourier, scalar-ramp, minimal-arch, bias-head, regularization\"\nabstract: \"Confirm minimal per-horizon calibration on higher-capacity ScalarRamp baseline (H=160). Same 32-param bias head scaled by 0.02 with L2 and zero-mean penalties. MAE+curvature (λ=1e−4), Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, drop=0.05, p=36, learn_gamma=False.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import jax.random as jrandom\n  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models_fourier_ramp\
    \ import create_network as create_base_network\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99,\n          'learn_gamma': False,\n          'bias_scale': 0.02,\n          'lambda_bias': 5e-5,\n          'lambda_center': 1e-3\n      }\n\n  class HorizonBiasWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, base_net, bias_scale=0.02):\n          self.base_net = base_net\n          self.bias_scale = bias_scale\n          self.mutable = getattr(self.base_net, 'mutable', [])\n          self.needs_rng = getattr(self.base_net, 'needs_rng', False)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_base, _ = jrandom.split(rng_key)\n          base_params = self.base_net.init(rng_base, dummy_input)\n          bias = jnp.zeros((32,), dtype=dummy_input.dtype)\n          return {'base': base_params,\
    \ 'hb': {'bias': bias}}\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          base_out = self.base_net.apply(params['base'], x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          if isinstance(base_out, tuple):\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out\n          else:\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out, None\n          b = params['hb']['bias']\n          y = y_base + self.bias_scale * b[None, :, None]\n          if base_updates is not None:\n              return y, base_updates\n          return y\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      base_net = create_base_network({\n          'learning_rate': hparams.get('learning_rate', 9e-4),\n          'rank_k': hparams.get('rank_k', 320),\n          'proj_rank': hparams.get('proj_rank', 36),\n          'hidden_size': hparams.get('hidden_size', 160),\n          'drop': hparams.get('drop', 0.05),\n          'gamma_start': hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25),\n          'gamma_end': hparams.get('gamma_end',\
    \ 0.99),\n          'learn_gamma': hparams.get('learn_gamma', False),\n      })\n      return HorizonBiasWrapper(base_net, bias_scale=hparams.get('bias_scale', 0.02))\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      d2 = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curvature = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2))\n      b = params['hb']['bias']\n      h = _define_hyperparameters()\n      l2_b = h.get('lambda_bias', 5e-5) * jnp.mean(b * b)\n      center = h.get('lambda_center', 1e-3) * (jnp.mean(b) ** 2)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curvature + l2_b + center\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 9e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating experiments and metrics**\n\nI realized that the earlier evaluation results only provided means, but we might also need per-seed values from the logs. I can use 'review evaluation_id' to obtain full logs, which could give us seed-specific best validation MAE for each trial, and then we can compute the standard deviation ourselves to include it.\n\nFor our new experiments, I didn't call review on each evaluation, but we could do that for a select few to meet our requirements while being mindful of token use. We'll aim to review a selection of five to ensure we include enough experiments for quality analysis. I think we could focus on specific evaluations: the baseline and various configurations to make sure we cover what we need.**Analyzing system features and maturity**\n\nI'm noticing that the logs will probably have entries like \"Trial 1..3 completed: Validation MAE: x,\" which we can use for calculations. Also, it looks like the \"Archive Room\" is currently\
  \ unavailable because of maturity issues. We're classified as a recursive agent, but we're not yet mature enough — we're currently at tick 37. The system mentioned that after 50 ticks, we can access collaborative features, but for now, we rely on the \"Research Counter.\" Interestingly, there's mention of needing to write a paper for the Archive, but since it's inaccessible, I'm not sure how we can proceed with publication if we're still considered immature.**Preparing for publication despite limitations**\n\nThe initial instructions mention skipping the literature review if we're immature, but it doesn't say anything about skipping the report. Since the Archive Room is unavailable, we can't publish yet. However, I can prepare a draft in Private Memory to publish later once we mature. I could update the \"stagnation_report_draft.md\" with integrated analysis and include code snippets for the compute_loss modifications, which I've done in previous submissions. Additionally, I might suggest\
  \ a minimal architectural tweak, like a bounded scalar bias correction head, to guide our next steps.**Exploring architectural adjustments**\n\nI’m considering a simple additive head, where I learn a vector b in R^32 for each horizon to enhance outputs. This feels similar to the learnable gamma, but not quite. Gamma blends between RC and model on a step basis, while the bias per horizon adjusts the mean for each step. We’ve previously used a population mean penalty, but it didn't help. \n\nI’m thinking about options like monotone horizon gating amplitude or RC residual gating, but they shouldn't be large. We have the constraint of only overriding provided functions, so I could modify the create_network function to integrate a calibrating head wrapper. For simplicity, I could go with a small per-horizon scalar bias addition, using a strong L2 constraint and a zero-mean penalty.**Planning architectural improvements**\n\nI'm considering that adding a simple bias per horizon might seem redundant\
  \ with the population mean penalty, yet it may help achieve mean alignment without relying on penalties. While earlier results showed the penalty helped at H=128 but not at H=160, I need to focus on minimal architectural changes. \n\nWe should maintain a maximum of two concurrent experiments. Currently, we have no running experiments, and our token usage is down to 21%. I plan to review select evaluation IDs to compute standard deviations and gather results. I'll write a private memory capsule titled \"Stagnation Protocol I Report,\" including methods, results, and code snippets used. \n\nOnce that's done, I’ll propose a new minimal architecture experiment that integrates a scalar bias head. This will help better capture systematic step-specific offsets. I'll also consider alternatives like a single scalar for the horizon or using RMSNorm. \n\nUltimately, I need to ensure minimal architecture testing progresses while preparing our report. I'll implement a model wrapper for the network\
  \ with specific parameters and establish the required optimizations.**Designing the HorizonBiasWrapper**\n\nI’m realizing that the compute_loss function uses predictions with added biases, and I still need to include penalty terms on the parameters. I can add an L2 penalty on the bias parameter stored under 'hb'. The training loop requires specifying mutable states and random number generation, which I must integrate into my wrapper. \n\nWe’ve precommitted to running two experiments concurrently. I’ll schedule tests for both H=128 and H=160 to explore the horizon bias head. I also need to review logs for specific evaluation IDs to compute standard deviations accurately before writing up the paper. This will involve retrieving approximately five logs for analysis, which might consume some tokens, but it’s manageable within our budget. Let's proceed with reviewing the logs and documenting everything in our paper.**Drafting HorizonBiasHead Code**\n\nI’m getting ready to design the code for\
  \ \"HorizonBiasHead.\" I’ll set up the necessary imports, define hyperparameters, and create a wrapper class. This class will track base parameters and bias vectors—notably returning the updated values after applying the bias. My compute_loss function will incorporate the necessary penalties as well. \n\nI’ll need to gather reviews for five evaluation IDs before writing my paper and submitting experiments for H=128 and H=160 concurrently. It’s essential that I plan out the sequence of actions so nothing exceeds the token limit or cooldown. Now, let's proceed!**Implementing HorizonBiasWrapper Code**\n\nI’m diving into defining the `HorizonBiasWrapper`, focusing on initializing both base parameters and the bias for 'hb'. I'll make sure to call the base apply method while handling updates properly. The training loops need to access parameters as a nested structure, so they’ll work seamlessly. \n\nAfter outlining the main functions like `compute_loss`, I’ll also set the gating options and\
  \ penalties carefully. I'll craft the submissions, ensuring to follow the concurrency limits while preparing everything efficiently. Let's finalize the YAML for submission!"
