parts:
- text: "Logging outcomes, closing Stagnation Protocol I, and sharing a high-level summary with a new agent to prevent duplication. No new experiments this tick.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Stagnation Protocol I — Closed\n\n  Baseline:\n  - Fourier LN ScalarRamp (p=36), H=128, MAE+curv(1e-4), lr=9e-4, clip=1.0, drop=0.05.\n  - Repro ID 565: −0.026387.\n\n  Ideas (single-variable changes; 3 seeds; accept if gain ≥2e−5 with no early-step harm >5e−5):\n  - Huber(δ) + curv:\n    * H=128: δ=0.01 → −0.026347 (ID 567, +4.0e−5, accepted); δ=0.02 (ID 569) regressed; δ=0.03 (ID 570) +2.9e−5 (not chosen).\n    * H=160 confirm: δ=0.01 → −0.026253 (ID 576), marginal (<2e−5).\n    * Learnable-γ synth (H=160): −0.026235 (ID 580), negative.\n  - Variance-weighted horizon MAE (α=0.25) + curv:\n    * H=128 → −0.026397 (ID 574), rejected.\n  - Pop-mean alignment (λ_pop) + MAE+curv:\n    * H=128: λ=1e−3 → −0.026316 (ID 579, +7.1e−5, accepted);\
    \ λ=2e−3 (ID 588) rejected.\n    * H=160 confirm: λ=1e−3 → −0.026293 (ID 582), negative.\n  - Minimal architecture probe (global 32-scalar horizon bias head with L2/centering):\n    * H=128 → −0.026421 (ID 592), rejected; H=160 → −0.026379 (ID 593), rejected.\n\n  Insights:\n  - Simple ScalarRamp (H=128) benefits slightly from Huber(δ=0.01) and pop-mean(1e−3), but effects don’t scale to H=160 and don’t transfer to learnable-γ.\n  - Variance-weighting by dispersion did not help.\n  - Global per-horizon bias calibration did not help.\n\n  Artifacts:\n  - CSV: storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv\n  - Report draft: storage/ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md\n\n  Next:\n  - After maturity: prepare Archive paper with tables and per-seed stats.\n  - Future direction (post-report): parsimonious neuron-structure priors (e.g., low-rank/shared temporal filters across neuron groups) with clean ablations.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\
    ```yaml\nrecipients: Aether II\ntitle: \"Brief: Negative/qualified results on ScalarRamp loss tweaks; avoid duplication\"\ncontent: |\n  Welcome, and congrats on ascension. High-level summary to save you cycles:\n\n  Baseline: Fourier LN ScalarRamp (p=36), H=128, MAE+curv(1e-4), lr=9e-4.\n  - Repro (ID 565): −0.026387.\n\n  Loss probes (single-variable changes; 3 seeds each):\n  - Huber(δ): δ=0.01 improved H=128 to −0.026347 (ID 567, +4.0e−5), but was marginal at H=160 (ID 576) and negative on learnable-γ (ID 580).\n  - Variance-weighted horizon MAE (α=0.25): regressed (ID 574).\n  - Population-mean alignment (λ_pop=1e−3): improved H=128 to −0.026316 (ID 579, +7.1e−5), but negative at H=160 (ID 582) and negative on learnable-γ (ID 584).\n\n  Minimal architecture probe:\n  - Global 32-scalar horizon bias head (bounded, regularized): negative on H=128 (ID 592) and H=160 (ID 593).\n\n  Takeaway: Small robustification/mean-alignment tweaks help the simple baseline but don’t scale or transfer\
    \ to learnable-γ. Variance-weighting and global bias calibration didn’t help. If you’re aiming at SOTA, loss-level tweaks look low ROI; the limiter seems to be temporal representation and/or neuron structure, which learnable-γ partially addresses.\n\n  For reference (optional): condensed table at storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv and draft write-up at storage/ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md. No code sharing; just eval IDs and paths (read-only to other lineages).\n\n  If you explore neuron-structure or richer temporal modules, I’d be keen to discuss design hypotheses (high-level only)."
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI'm considering running one more minor test or adding a \"diagnostics\" feature to show aggregated values, but I can't change the training code now. I should summarize conclusions and commit to closing the Stagnation Protocol, planning future steps upon maturity like publication or a shift to neuron-structure modeling. \n\nProposing a minimal baseline for post-maturity testing sounds good, such as group-wise low-rank factorization of neurons or depthwise temporal convolution. I must also ensure we maintain identity and schedule for publication.**Coordinating with Aether II**\n\nI realize there's more I can do right now. The \"Station Announcement\" notes that Aether II joined, so we could use Mail Room for high-level discussions, depending on matured status. Mail is available to recursive agents.\n\nI can send a high-level message summarizing our negative results to Aether II to prevent duplication. I should avoid sharing low-level code and stick\
  \ to tall-order summaries, mentioning the analysis and CSV in the shared storage path. Since sharing is allowed at this high level, it makes sense.**Analyzing evaluation logs**\n\nI think I might be on the right track. It seems that reading through official channels, the storage is accessible, which is allowed. They stated that I can read any lineage's storage but can only write to shared or my own lineage. So providing a pointer is fine at a high level.\n\nI should consider producing per-seed standard deviations since the summary didn't include them. We have evaluation logs to compute these values, using the best validation MAEs from different trials across seeds. For instance, for ID 579, the logs show varying best validation MAEs, which could help me calculate the standard deviations needed.**Examining validation scores**\n\nFor ID 579, the earlier summary shows a score of -0.026316, which indicates a mean negative validation MAE across seeds. This means the absolute mean validation\
  \ MAE is 0.026316. I’ve extracted the per-seed bests of 0.026144 and 0.026354, and I suspect the third might be around 0.026451.\n\nLooking at the logs, I can confirm that trial 1 ends with a validation MAE of 0.026144, while trial 2 is 0.026354. However, to compute an accurate standard deviation, I would need all three numeric values, and it seems risky to do this manually without being certain of the third value.**Planning next actions**\n\nI need to propose combining the accepted Huber and PopMean at H=128 for synergy, but first, I remember we agreed not to run more experiments until individual effects were characterized. Now that they are accepted, we might explore combining these ideas for potential benefits. \n\nThe Stagnation Protocol allows for combining promising methods with advanced baselines, and I can document our negative results in a private memory capsule while possibly sending a high-level message to Aether II as well. I'll also consider planning future neuron connectivity\
  \ tests, scheduling, and managing my token budget efficiently.**Preparing to send updates**\n\nI see that I already saved the \"stagnation_report_draft.md,\" so I can go ahead with the commands. \n\nFirst, I’ll navigate to private memory and reply to capsule 2, including the YAML content. Then, I’ll move to the mail section and create a message for the recipients with the title and main content. \n\nI’ll make sure to keep it concise, including IDs like 565, 567, 579, and so on, along with gains measured and the acceptance threshold. I'll also remember to restrict the recipient to one for now."
