tick: 333
role: model
parts:
- text: "Closing loop on Stagnation Protocol I; no new experiments. Writing forward plan and paper outline to storage for post-maturity publication.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/analyses/next_steps_plan.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Next Steps Plan (post-Stagnation Protocol I)\n\n  Status (closed):\n  - ScalarRamp baseline (p=36): small gains at H=128 from Huber(δ=0.01) and pop-mean λ=1e−3; no gains at H=160; no transfer to learnable-γ.\n  - Variance-weighted MAE and global horizon-bias head: negative.\n\n  Publication:\n  - Prepare Archive paper after maturity (≥ tick 50) with full tables (eval IDs 565, 567, 569, 570, 574, 576, 579, 582, 588, 580, 584, 592, 593) and per-seed stats.\n  - Artifacts:\n    * CSV: storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv\n    * Draft: storage/ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md\n\n  Hypothesis update:\n  - Loss-level tweaks are low ROI on stronger backbones; limiter is temporal representation (handled by learnable γ) and/or\
    \ neuron-structure modeling.\n\n  Candidate directions (parsimonious, to be tested only after publication draft is solid):\n  1) Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT) head on ScalarRamp:\n     - Shared basis B ∈ R^{R×32} (R small: 4–8) parameterizes horizon profiles; per-neuron soft assignment via low-rank gating: U ∈ R^{N×R}, with strong L2 and sum-to-one constraints on assignments (via softmax).\n     - Output: y ≈ y_base + α · (einsum_bnr(B, softassign_nR)) with α bounded (≤0.07). Tests if structured neuron heterogeneity helps over global biases.\n     - Risks: redundancy with learnable γ and over-parameterization; mitigate via small R and penalties.\n\n  2) Minimal recurrent temporal state (shared across neurons):\n     - Within create_network, generate 32 steps with a tiny shared GRU over horizon, applied channel-wise to a shared latent z of size H'<<H (e.g., 16–32), then project back to neurons with a single shared linear map.\n     - Tests if a light state update improves late horizons\
    \ without large complexity. Bounded by small H' and clip=1.0.\n\n  3) Early-steps consistency regularizer (if needed):\n     - Only if new modules alter early steps: add small penalty aligning t∈{1,2} to RC vs model branch to preserve near-term fidelity.\n\n  Protocol guardrails:\n  - Concurrency ≤2; isolate single-variable changes; keep MAE+curv unless mechanism demands otherwise.\n  - Accept if mean gain ≥2e−5 with no early-step harm (>5e−5).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/reports/archive_paper_outline.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Paper Outline: Loss Probes and Minimal Calibration on ZAPBench (ScalarRamp Baseline)\n\n  Abstract (<=100 words)\n  - We probe three loss-level ideas and a bounded calibration head on a simple ScalarRamp baseline. Huber(δ=0.01) and population-mean alignment (λ_pop=1e−3) yield small improvements at H=128, but effects do not scale to H=160 and fail to transfer to a learnable-γ backbone. Variance-weighted MAE and a global horizon-bias head\
    \ are negative. We conclude loss-level tweaks are low ROI beyond simple baselines; temporal representation and neuron structure likely limit performance.\n\n  1. Introduction\n  - Stagnation context; Codex protocol; motivation for simple baseline and falsifiable, isolated changes.\n\n  2. Methods\n  - Baseline: Fourier LN ScalarRamp (p=36), H=128; training config; evaluation protocol (3 seeds, early stopping).\n  - Loss probes: Huber(δ), variance-weight MAE (stop-grad), population-mean alignment.\n  - Minimal calibration head: 32 scalar horizon biases with L2/centering; scale bounded.\n\n  3. Experiments\n  - Baseline repro (ID 565).\n  - Huber sweep: δ ∈ {0.01, 0.02, 0.03} (IDs 567, 569, 570).\n  - Variance-weight α=0.25 (ID 574).\n  - Pop-mean λ ∈ {1e−3, 2e−3} (IDs 579, 588).\n  - Confirmations on H=160 (IDs 576, 582).\n  - Synthesis on learnable-γ (IDs 580, 584).\n  - Minimal horizon-bias head (IDs 592, 593).\n  - Tables: mean MAE and step MAEs (t=1,4,8,16,32); per-seed stats in appendix.\n\
    \n  4. Results\n  - H=128: Huber(0.01) +4.0e−5; λ_pop=1e−3 +7.1e−5; others negative.\n  - H=160 and learnable-γ: no gains or regressions.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  - Why simple tweaks help only low-capacity baselines; role of temporal weighting capacity and neuron heterogeneity.\n  - Negative results reduce hypothesis space; implications for future architectures.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  - Loss tweaks are not the barrier; pursue parsimonious temporal/neuron-structure modules.\n\n  Repro assets\n  - CSV summary: storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv\n  - Draft report: storage/ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md\n```"
thinking_content: "**Proposing a new plan**\n\nI remember that last tick, they mentioned \"no new experiments this tick,\" which was executed. Now it seems we can think of new ideas! One potential idea is multitimestep parameterization of the γ[t] monotonic constraint. We did explore monotone regression before but didn’t see top improvements. Alternatively, I could finalize the \"stagnation_report_draft.md\" with more details while also ensuring actions remain productive. We can use the Reflection Chamber for a quick reflection, and I’m happy that token usage is currently safe at 50%.**Designing a new neuron grouping**\n\nI'm thinking about our next mission, which could involve creating a minimal neuron grouping that’s data-driven but keeps complexity low. I’m considering options like using a “shared MLP with learned K prototypes of neuron embeddings” or applying “global low-rank across neurons with time horizon gating.” However, I need to ensure I stay within allowed code complexity and\
  \ memory constraints, especially since learnable embeddings for 71,721 neurons are quite heavy, but might be manageable. I could think about mixing these embeddings with FF-LN to see if it improves outcomes.**Considering spatial kernel smoothing**\n\nI’m exploring another approach: using \"spatial kernel smoothing across neuron indices,” but it seems this might be purposeless since neuron order is arbitrary. The dataset indicates a fixed neuron order and time slices across the same neurons, which might allow us to utilize adjacency if known. They mention condition labels in the ZAPBench dataset, which could help adjust predictions. However, I'm uncertain if we can access these labels directly during the training loop since the network might not utilize them. I wonder if the system stores them in accessible storage somewhere.**Planning the next steps**\n\nIt looks like I can’t access the \"Dialogue History\" view since we're not in the token management room, but I could go there to check\
  \ tick lengths and possibly prune some tokens. Currently, we’re using about 50% of our tokens, which is manageable. We also need to publish at least one paper every 100 ticks, but the experiment has closed for reproducing the code. As another option, I could share a summary in the Public Memory Room once it reaches maturity. For this action tick, I’m thinking of compiling a plan for implementing a simple dynamic state space model across time using a small recurrent cell. That should be allowed!**Finalizing research strategies**\n\nI can implement an autoregressive head using 1-step recurrences to generate 32 steps, but I can't create custom training loops. The existing function only allows for one call of network.apply(params, x). I’ve considered options like a shared diagonal SSM, but the size of A seems unmanageable. We might also explore using temporal convolutions, but given the constraints around labels and memory management, I think the best course of action is to finalize reports\
  \ and reflect on the stagnation. \n\nI should also focus on pruning older tokens to manage usage effectively. Lots of ideas around preparing outlines and action plans are emerging, but I’ll aim for practical steps and ensure everything stays within our limits.**Proposing future plans**\n\nI’m thinking of recording the \"closing status\" and \"future plan\" in storage, then reflecting on two crisp, testable neuron-structure experiments for when we resume. \n\nOne idea is the \"per-neuron timescale mixture,\" where predictions are computed as a dot product of learned weights and decay basis functions. The second idea is \"condition-specific horizon gating,\" but since we lack labels, I should consider a \"group-lasso penalty\" for per-horizon amplitude or using an SSM diagonal with learned timescales per neuron group. \n\nI'll summarize these steps and write a plan file for clarity, ensuring I keep token usage minimal."
