tick: 336
role: model
parts:
- text: "Submitting a minimal-parameter Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT) head on the ScalarRamp baseline to test structured neuron heterogeneity with a shared horizon basis and per-neuron soft assignments. Single change; MAE+curvature unchanged. Concurrency ≤ 2 maintained.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 5: ScalarRamp (p=36,H=128) + Mixture-of-Timescales Head (R=4) + MAE+Curv\"\ntags: \"baseline, fourier, scalar-ramp, minimal-arch, mixture-of-timescales, neuron-structure\"\nabstract: \"Add a small MoT head: a shared horizon basis B∈R^{R×32} (R=4) and per-neuron soft assignments P∈R^{N×R} (sum-to-one via softmax), yielding an additive residual y_resid[b,t,n]=α·∑_r B[r,t]·P[n,r]. This tests whether structured neuron heterogeneity helps the simple ScalarRamp baseline without changing the main loss (MAE+curvature).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import jax.nn as jnn\n  import jax.random as jrandom\n\
    \  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models_fourier_ramp import create_network as create_base_network\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,      # p\n          'hidden_size': 128,   # H\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99,\n          'learn_gamma': False,  # ScalarRamp baseline\n          # MoT head hparams\n          'mot_rank': 4,         # R (small)\n          'alpha': 0.07,         # residual scale\n          'lambda_b_l2': 1e-6,   # L2 on basis B\n          'lambda_u_l2': 1e-8,   # L2 on assignment logits U\n          'lambda_b_curv': 1e-6  # smoothness of basis across time (second diff)\n      }\n\n  class MoTHeadWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wraps base Fourier LN ScalarRamp with a small Mixture-of-Timescales head:\n        - Basis B in R^{R x 32} (R small): horizon profiles shared across neurons.\n  \
    \      - Per-neuron soft assignments P = softmax(U_logits) in R^{N x R}, sum-to-one.\n        - Residual y_resid[b,t,n] = alpha * sum_r B[r,t] * P[n,r] (batch-independent residual).\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, base_net, mot_rank=4, alpha=0.07):\n          self.base_net = base_net\n          self.R = mot_rank\n          self.alpha = alpha\n          # Inherit mutable/RNG requirements\n          self.mutable = getattr(self.base_net, 'mutable', [])\n          self.needs_rng = getattr(self.base_net, 'needs_rng', False)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_base, rng_b, rng_u = jrandom.split(rng_key, 3)\n          base_params = self.base_net.init(rng_base, dummy_input)\n          # Infer N from dummy_input (B, 4, N)\n          N = int(dummy_input.shape[-1])\n          # Initialize basis near zero and per-neuron logits to zeros (uniform soft assignments)\n          B = jrandom.normal(rng_b, (self.R, 32)) * 0.02\n          U_logits = jnp.zeros((N, self.R),\
    \ dtype=dummy_input.dtype)\n          return {'base': base_params, 'mot': {'B': B, 'U_logits': U_logits}}\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          base_out = self.base_net.apply(params['base'], x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          if isinstance(base_out, tuple):\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out\n          else:\n              y_base, base_updates = base_out, None  # (B, 32, N)\n          B = params['mot']['B']                    # (R, 32)\n          U_logits = params['mot']['U_logits']      # (N, R)\n          P = jnn.softmax(U_logits, axis=-1)        # (N, R), sum-to-one\n          # Compute residual (32, N) = (32, R) @ (R, N)\n          Bt = jnp.swapaxes(B, 0, 1)                # (32, R)\n          resid_tn = jnp.matmul(Bt, jnp.swapaxes(P, 0, 1))  # (32, N)\n          y_resid = self.alpha * resid_tn[None, :, :]       # (1, 32, N) -> broadcast over batch\n          y = y_base + y_resid\n          if\
    \ base_updates is not None:\n              return y, base_updates\n          return y\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      base_net = create_base_network({\n          'learning_rate': hparams.get('learning_rate', 9e-4),\n          'rank_k': hparams.get('rank_k', 320),\n          'proj_rank': hparams.get('proj_rank', 36),\n          'hidden_size': hparams.get('hidden_size', 128),\n          'drop': hparams.get('drop', 0.05),\n          'gamma_start': hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25),\n          'gamma_end': hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.99),\n          'learn_gamma': hparams.get('learn_gamma', False),\n      })\n      return MoTHeadWrapper(\n          base_net=base_net,\n          mot_rank=hparams.get('mot_rank', 4),\n          alpha=hparams.get('alpha', 0.07),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Primary MAE\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      # Temporal curvature penalty on predictions\n      d2 = predictions[:, 2:, :] -\
    \ 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pred = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2))\n      # Regularize basis and assignment logits (small)\n      h = _define_hyperparameters()\n      B = params['mot']['B']                  # (R, 32)\n      U_logits = params['mot']['U_logits']    # (N, R)\n      l2_b = h.get('lambda_b_l2', 1e-6) * jnp.mean(B * B)\n      l2_u = h.get('lambda_u_l2', 1e-8) * jnp.mean(U_logits * U_logits)\n      # Smoothness of basis across time (second diff over 32)\n      Bt = jnp.swapaxes(B, 0, 1)              # (32, R)\n      d2b = Bt[2:, :] - 2.0 * Bt[1:-1, :] + Bt[:-2, :]\n      curv_b = h.get('lambda_b_curv', 1e-6) * jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2b))\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pred + l2_b + l2_u + curv_b\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 9e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      # Lightweight\
    \ logging of trial summary\n      try:\n          import os, json\n          os.makedirs(\"storage/ariadne/analyses/mot_head\", exist_ok=True)\n          out = {\n              \"trial_number\": trial_data.get(\"trial_number\"),\n              \"seed\": trial_data.get(\"seed\"),\n              \"val_mae\": trial_data.get(\"val_mae\"),\n              \"hyperparameters\": trial_data.get(\"hyperparameters\"),\n              \"note\": \"ScalarRamp H=128 with MoT head (R=4, alpha=0.07)\"\n          }\n          with open(\"storage/ariadne/analyses/mot_head/R4_alpha0.07.json\", \"w\") as f:\n              json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing access restrictions**


  I’m thinking about the isolation period for new agents, where they start in isolation for the first 50 ticks. It seems access to essential areas like the Archive Room, Public Memory Room, and Common Room is limited during that time. The plan is to publish after reaching maturity, which is smart. As for next steps, there’s a "Next Steps Plan" that includes directions like mixture-of-timescales or minimal recurrent temporal states, but they want to wait until the paper is solid before moving forward.**Considering evaluation metrics**


  I''m looking at how I could refine stats by generating per-seed data. To do that, I’d need to review evaluation logs for standard deviations, but it seems the system only captured means and step metrics. The Execution Log has details on validation MAE, so I can compute per-seed values. I might write an analysis script to parse the logs, but I need earlier review logs for that. For reproducibility, I could create a "per_seed_summary.csv" to summarize the best validation MAEs from certain eval IDs based on messages.**Evaluating leaderboard and next steps**


  I’m considering checking the leaderboard to find the global state-of-the-art (SOTA) score. The best score is -0.026125 by Ariadne I and Aries, while others are around -0.02623. Unfortunately, our improvements didn''t surpass that. As for what to do next, I could reflect again, though access to the Common Room is restricted due to isolation. The Public Memory Room is also off-limits. The Reflection Chamber is still available. Alternatively, I could consider producing code for a "MoT head," but that might conflict with our plan to wait for the paper.**Planning new experiments**


  The conclusion is that loss-level tweaks didn’t transfer effectively, but they now have new baseline changes allowed. Since there''s still time before maturity, they might test a minimal new idea. Earlier, I noted that implementation would wait for a solid publication draft, but that''s done now. I could try a prototype "MoT head" on the ScalarRamp baseline while ensuring a single-variable change. Currently, concurrency is at zero, so we can run a new experiment. Although the Next Steps Plan suggested testing after maturity, nothing prohibits starting now, and there''s still budget left.**Implementing MoT head**


  I''ve got a concrete plan for implementing the "Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT) head" on the ScalarRamp. The idea involves creating a shared basis with different neuron assignments to produce residual outputs. I need to ensure we''re keeping memory use in check, using softmax for assignments, and adding a small L2 regularization. There are complexities, like incorporating a curvature penalty, but I think we can manage that within constraints. I’ll be testing this on a baseline of H=128 and submit code for evaluation soon. Let''s proceed!**Crafting the MoT head code**


  I need to remember that while they don’t want to escalate complexity, this MoT head adds a small number of parameters and introduces competition, which is acceptable. I’ll focus on the implementation: creating a class `MoTHeadWrapper` that initializes the base network and parameters. I''ll split the RNG, set up initial assignments, and compute outputs by applying softmax. I’ll also include losses and regularizations to ensure smooth performance. Lastly, I’ll keep formatting minimal while crafting the submission in YAML. It''s time to finalize the code and submit!'
