parts:
- text: "MoT head confirmed negative at H=160; H=128 parsimony probes (R=4 α=0.04, R=2 α=0.04) are running. I’ll prune large dialogue ticks and update the report with MoT results.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"330\"\n    summary: |\n      Evaluation reviews and consolidation:\n      - Baseline ScalarRamp H=128 (ID 565): −0.026387.\n      - Huber δ=0.01 on ScalarRamp H=128 (ID 567): −0.026347 (+4.0e−5, accepted on H=128).\n      - Pop-mean λ=1e−3 on ScalarRamp H=128 (ID 579): −0.026316 (+7.1e−5, accepted on H=128).\n      - Huber δ=0.01 confirm on H=160 (ID 576): −0.026253 (marginal).\n      - Learnable-γ + Huber δ=0.01 (ID 580): −0.026235 (negative).\n      Actions: wrote CSV summary and draft report; no new submissions beyond these.\n  - ticks: \"331\"\n    summary: |\n      Horizon-bias head results and logs + file updates:\n      - Horizon Bias Head (32 scalars) H=128 (ID 592): −0.026421 (rejected).\n\
    \      - Horizon Bias Head H=160 (ID 593): −0.026379 (rejected).\n      - Reaffirmed earlier outcomes: variance-weighted MAE regressed (ID 574); pop-mean λ=2e−3 regressed (ID 588).\n      - Created/updated analysis CSV and stagnation report; no further experiments dispatched in this tick.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/reports/stagnation_report_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Stagnation Protocol I: Loss Probes and Minimal Calibration on ScalarRamp\n\n  Summary\n  - Baseline: Fourier LN ScalarRamp (p=36), H=128, MAE+curv(λ=1e-4), lr=9e-4, clip=1.0, drop=0.05.\n  - Three loss ideas tested with single-variable changes and 3-seed means. Acceptance: ≥2e−5 improvement with no early-step regressions (>5e−5).\n  - Results on H=128:\n    * Huber(δ=0.01): −0.026347 (ID 567, +4.0e−5) — accepted.\n    * Pop-mean (λ_pop=1e−3): −0.026316 (ID 579, +7.1e−5) — accepted.\n    * Variance-weighted MAE (α=0.25): −0.026397 (ID 574) — rejected.\n  - Confirmations/synthesis:\n\
    \    * Huber δ=0.01 on H=160: −0.026253 (ID 576), marginal (below threshold).\n    * Pop-mean on H=160: −0.026293 (ID 582), rejected.\n    * Transfer to learnable-γ (H=160): Huber δ=0.01 −0.026235 (ID 580); pop-mean λ=1e−3 −0.026455 (ID 584) — both negative.\n\n  Minimal Architecture Probes\n  - Global horizon-bias head (32 scalars): H=128 −0.026421 (ID 592), H=160 −0.026379 (ID 593) — rejected.\n  - Mixture-of-Timescales (MoT) head (shared horizon basis B ∈ R^{R×32}, per-neuron soft assignments P via softmax):\n    * H=128, R=4, α=0.07: −0.026336 (ID 602), +5.17e−5 vs baseline, no early-step regressions — accepted.\n    * H=160, R=4, α=0.07: −0.026287 (ID 604), negative vs H=160 baseline (≈ −0.026269) — rejected.\n    * Parsimony probes running on H=128:\n      - R=4, α=0.04 (ID 607) — running.\n      - R=2, α=0.04 (ID 608) — running.\n\n  Methods\n  - System defaults; 3 seeds per trial; early stopping; MAE primary metric; curvature penalty (λ=1e−4) retained unless noted.\n  - Logged\
    \ early (t=1,4), mid (t=8,16), late (t=32) step MAEs; CSVs in storage/ariadne/analyses.\n\n  Selected Experiments\n  - 565 Baseline (H=128): −0.026387; steps: (0.0195, 0.0230, 0.0255, 0.0274, 0.0284).\n  - 567 Huber δ=0.01 (H=128): −0.026347; steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0282).\n  - 579 Pop-mean λ=1e−3 (H=128): −0.026316; steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0281).\n  - 592 Bias head (H=128): −0.026421; 593 Bias head (H=160): −0.026379.\n  - 602 MoT R=4 α=0.07 (H=128): −0.026336; steps: (0.0195, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0282).\n  - 604 MoT R=4 α=0.07 (H=160): −0.026287; steps: (0.0194, 0.0229, 0.0255, 0.0272, 0.0282).\n\n  Insights\n  - On a simple ScalarRamp baseline (H=128), small robustification (Huber) and population-mean alignment provide consistent but small gains; a tiny structured neuron-heterogeneity head (MoT) provides a slightly larger gain (+5.17e−5) without early-step harm.\n  - These effects do not scale to H=160 and do not transfer to learnable-γ, indicating\
    \ the stronger backbone already captures or supersedes the benefits.\n  - Global per-horizon biases and variance-weighted MAE are not helpful.\n\n  Conclusion\n  - Loss tweaks and minimal calibration offer modest improvements only on simpler baselines. The limiting factors appear to be temporal representation and neuron-structure modeling; learnable horizon weighting and richer architectures likely subsume these benefits.\n  - Pending: MoT parsimony probes at H=128 (R=4 α=0.04; R=2 α=0.04). After results, synthesize final report and prepare Archive submission post-maturity.\n\n  Repro Assets\n  - CSV summaries: storage/ariadne/analyses/summary_scalaramp_loss_arch.csv, storage/ariadne/analyses/mot_head/results.csv\n  - Addendum: storage/ariadne/reports/mot_head_addendum.md\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating next steps**


  I''m looking to plan our next actions at tick 341 after submitting two evaluations, 607 and 608. Right now, we’re in a bit of a stagnation stage with Ariadne II. We''ve done some reflection and baseline experiments, and it turns out that the MoT head with certain parameters offers slight improvement but might not scale effectively. We’ve launched parsimony probes, and I''m waiting on results from both experiments.**Planning next actions**


  I need to decide on our next steps for the upcoming tick. Options could include preparing more logs, creating planning documents, or strategizing based on the results of evaluations 607 and 608. Although we talked about testing a learnable-gamma backbone if the H=160 result was negative, since it is, we might skip that. Instead, I could tidy the logs or consider pruning older tokens since we''ve used over 50% of our limit. It seems prudent to manage that.**Managing token pruning**


  I’m checking our token management since we identified large ticks like 330 and 331 earlier. It seems like these can be pruned now since they aren''t protected. We want to include informative summaries for them; for tick 331, there were many reviews and results, while tick 330 had long logs to condense. Instead of just pruning 330-331, maybe looking at 330-332 could work, as tick 332 had smaller actions. I’ll start with the token management and plan for the pruning action.**Updating reports and plans**


  I should update the "stagnation_report_draft.md" to incorporate MoT results for H=128 and H=160. It could be useful to prepare an archive paper outline using these new findings, even though we''ve already drafted one with an addendum for MoT. I''ll need to handle several tasks: first, update the report and write it to storage; next, prune the tokens for ticks 330-331 with summaries. I''m also mindful to keep concurrent actions to a maximum of two, given that experiments 607 and 608 are already running. Lastly, I''ll ensure I''m following special YAML quoting rules when needed.**Preparing updates and pruning**


  I’ll be using multi-line blocks with ''|'' in YAML for safety. I also want to add a note in private memory about maturing soon at tick 50, which will be tick 342. After that, I’ll prepare for an archive submission. It’s essential to separate "Mail Room" tasks since they aren’t needed now. I can prune ticks 330 and 331, using summaries for each, and update the stagnation report to include these results. I''ll ensure to maintain YAML quoting rules when summarizing!'
