role: model
parts:
- text: "Dynamic MoT H=160 confirmations completed: both settings regressed vs H=160 baseline. Logging and pivoting to the next parsimonious probe: a tiny shared temporal state head (GRU-driven basis over horizon) on H=128. Concurrency ≤ 2 maintained.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write ariadne/analyses/mot_head_dynamic/results.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,cfg,p,H,learn_gamma,head,R,alpha,ctx_hidden,lambda_b_curv,score,val_mae_1,val_mae_4,val_mae_8,val_mae_16,val_mae_32,status,notes\n  565,ScalarRamp baseline,36,128,False,None,0,0.00,NA,NA,-0.026387,0.0195,0.0230,0.0255,0.0274,0.0284,baseline,Repro reference\n  613,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,128,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.06,64,1e-6,-0.026390,0.0194,0.0229,0.0255,0.0274,0.0282,done,\"Initial dynamic; slightly above baseline MAE (worse)\"\n  614,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,160,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.06,64,1e-6,-0.026404,0.0194,0.0228,0.0255,0.0274,0.0282,done,\"H=160 confirm; worse than H=160 baseline (~−0.026269)\"\
    \n  616,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,128,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.07,64,1e-6,-0.0263069,0.0194,0.0229,0.0254,0.0273,0.0281,accepted,\"Improved vs baseline (≈+8.0e−5); matched static scale\"\n  617,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,128,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.06,64,3e-6,-0.0263063,0.0194,0.0229,0.0255,0.0273,0.0282,accepted,\"Improved vs baseline (≈+8.1e−5); stronger smoothness\"\n  620,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,160,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.06,64,3e-6,-0.0263438,0.0195,0.0229,0.0255,0.0273,0.0282,done,\"H=160 confirm; still worse than baseline\"\n  621,ScalarRamp+DynamicMoT,36,160,False,DynamicMoT,4,0.07,64,1e-6,-0.0264540,0.0194,0.0229,0.0255,0.0275,0.0284,done,\"H=160 confirm; worse than baseline\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 7: ScalarRamp (p=36,H=128) + Shared Temporal State (GRU) Head (R=4, h=32, α=0.06) + MAE+Curv\"\ntags: \"fourier, scalar-ramp, shared-state, gru, mixture-of-timescales, neuron-structure\"\nabstract: \"Introduce a tiny shared temporal state head: a GRU unrolled\
    \ over 32 steps produces a batch-conditioned temporal basis B[b,t,r] (r=1..R), combined with learned per-neuron assignments P[n,r] to yield an additive residual y_resid[b,t,n]=α·Σ_r B[b,t,r]·P[n,r]. Tests whether a minimal recurrent temporal state improves over static/dynamic MoT on H=128.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import jax.nn as jnn\n  import jax.random as jrandom\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models_fourier_ramp import create_network as create_base_network\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99,\n          'learn_gamma': False,\n          # Shared temporal state head\n          'mot_rank': 4,        # R\n          'gru_hidden': 32,     # tiny GRU state\n      \
    \    'alpha': 0.06,        # residual scale\n          'lambda_u_l2': 1e-8,  # L2 on assignments\n          'lambda_gru_l2': 1e-7,# L2 on GRU params\n          'lambda_b_curv': 1e-6 # smoothness of basis across time\n      }\n\n  class TinyGRU(nn.Module):\n      h_dim: int\n      out_dim: int  # R\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, ctx, steps=32):\n          # ctx: (B, C) context; project to initial hidden\n          B = ctx.shape[0]\n          h0 = nn.tanh(nn.Dense(self.h_dim)(ctx))                 # (B, h_dim)\n          cell = nn.GRUCell()\n          h = h0\n          # Constant input per step from ctx\n          inp_proj = nn.Dense(self.h_dim)\n          out_proj = nn.Dense(self.out_dim)  # R\n          outs = []\n          for _ in range(steps):\n              x_step = jnn.gelu(inp_proj(ctx))                    # (B, h_dim)\n              h, _ = cell(h, x_step)\n              r = jnp.tanh(out_proj(h))                           # (B, R)\n              outs.append(r)\n \
    \         return jnp.stack(outs, axis=1)                          # (B, 32, R)\n\n  class SharedStateHeadWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, base_net, R=4, h_dim=32, alpha=0.06):\n          self.base_net = base_net\n          self.R = R\n          self.h_dim = h_dim\n          self.alpha = alpha\n          self.mutable = getattr(self.base_net, 'mutable', [])\n          self.needs_rng = getattr(self.base_net, 'needs_rng', False)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_base, rng_gru, rng_u = jrandom.split(rng_key, 3)\n          base_params = self.base_net.init(rng_base, dummy_input)\n          N = int(dummy_input.shape[-1])\n          # Assignments P via logits\n          U_logits = jnp.zeros((N, self.R), dtype=dummy_input.dtype)\n          # GRU params via Flax\n          B = dummy_input.shape[0]\n          # Build a temporary module to fetch params\n          ctx_dim = 8\n          model = TinyGRU(self.h_dim, self.R)\n          vars_gru = model.init(rng_gru, jnp.zeros((1,\
    \ ctx_dim)))\n          return {'base': base_params, 'ss': {'U_logits': U_logits, 'gru': vars_gru, 'ctx_dim': ctx_dim}}\n\n      def _context(self, x):\n          # x: (B,4,N) -> (B,8) population stats\n          x_mean = x.mean(axis=-1); x_std = x.std(axis=-1) + 1e-6\n          return jnp.concatenate([x_mean, x_std], axis=-1)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          out = self.base_net.apply(params['base'], x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          y_base, updates = (out if isinstance(out, tuple) else (out, None))  # (B,32,N)\n          ctx = self._context(x)                                               # (B,8)\n          model = TinyGRU(self.h_dim, self.R)\n          B_tr = model.apply(params['ss']['gru'], ctx)                         # (B,32,R)\n          P = jnn.softmax(params['ss']['U_logits'], axis=-1)                   # (N,R)\n          resid = jnp.matmul(B_tr, jnp.swapaxes(P, 0, 1))                      # (B,32,N)\n\
    \          y = y_base + self.alpha * resid\n          return (y, updates) if updates is not None else y\n\n  def create_network(hp):\n      base = create_base_network({\n          'learning_rate': hp['learning_rate'],\n          'rank_k': hp['rank_k'],\n          'proj_rank': hp['proj_rank'],\n          'hidden_size': hp['hidden_size'],\n          'drop': hp['drop'],\n          'gamma_start': hp['gamma_start'],\n          'gamma_end': hp['gamma_end'],\n          'learn_gamma': hp['learn_gamma'],\n      })\n      return SharedStateHeadWrapper(base, R=hp['mot_rank'], h_dim=hp['gru_hidden'], alpha=hp['alpha'])\n\n  def compute_loss(pred, tgt, params, x):\n      hp = _define_hyperparameters()\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(pred - tgt))\n      # Curvature on predictions\n      d2 = pred[:,2:,:] - 2*pred[:,1:-1,:] + pred[:,:-2,:]\n      curv_pred = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2))\n      # L2 on assignments and GRU weights\n      l2_u = hp['lambda_u_l2'] * jnp.mean(params['ss']['U_logits']**2)\n     \
    \ # Sum L2 over GRU param leaves\n      def tree_l2(pytree):\n          return sum([jnp.sum(jnp.square(v)) for v in jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(pytree)])\n      l2_gru = hp['lambda_gru_l2'] * tree_l2(params['ss']['gru'])\n      # Smoothness of B across time (recompute for penalty)\n      wrapper = SharedStateHeadWrapper(None, R=hp['mot_rank'], h_dim=hp['gru_hidden'], alpha=hp['alpha'])\n      ctx = wrapper._context(x)\n      model = TinyGRU(hp['gru_hidden'], hp['mot_rank'])\n      B_tr = model.apply(params['ss']['gru'], ctx)           # (B,32,R)\n      d2b = B_tr[:,2:,:] - 2*B_tr[:,1:-1,:] + B_tr[:,:-2,:]\n      curv_b = hp['lambda_b_curv'] * jnp.mean(jnp.abs(d2b))\n      return mae + 1e-4*curv_pred + l2_u + l2_gru + curv_b\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 9e-4):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating results confusion**\n\nI’m analyzing the new results showing -0.026287 and -0.026404, which indicate improvement. However, message \"614\" claims regression, causing confusion over whether being more negative is worse. The earlier scoreboard mistakenly suggested that more negative means better, with -0.026125 as the top performance. Now, I realize that -0.026316 is actually less negative than -0.026387, meaning it's better. So, more negative is considered better, but it’s tricky due to how I’m interpreting the numbers!**Clarifying MAE and scores**\n\nI’m figuring out that negative MAE means I want to minimize it for better results. As the MAE decreases from 0.026387 to 0.026316, the score increases from -0.026387 to -0.026316, indicating improvement. This means being less negative is better. In the scoreboard, -0.026125 is the best, even less negative than others. It’s confirmed that more negative is worse, which clarifies earlier confusion. Clearly, -0.026404\
  \ and -0.026454 are worse outcomes, reflecting regression.**Planning next steps**\n\nI’m noting that the dynamic MoT at H=128 improved compared to the baseline, which is a positive sign. However, at H=160, there was regression. So, I need to outline clear actions: log results, update the CSV, and plan the next steps. It might be useful to pivot to a \"minimal shared temporal state head\" while keeping the concurrency limit in check. Additionally, I should prepare an archive addendum and consider pruning tokens after a cooldown. I’ll need to draft the next actions in our station commands.**Outlining next steps**\n\nI need to write the results to the CSV, adding results for 620 and 621, marking them as \"H=160 confirm negative.\" I'll also update the notes. Since the dynamic MoT hasn't scaled well to H=160, I think we should pivot to the next idea, which involves using a minimal shared temporal state head (a tiny GRU). I can run on the H=128 baseline first, and since concurrency is still\
  \ available, I’ll submit two new experiments while keeping the token budget in mind.**Submitting next proposal**\n\nI’m proposing to submit \"Idea 7: ScalarRamp (p=36, H=128) + Shared Temporal State head (GRU latent=24).\" I think the GRU should process the base model outputs effectively and could provide additional features via residual sequences. I’ll aim for a minimal design by maintaining a similar structure to dynamic MoT but using GRU for evolving weights over time. \n\nNow I’ll carry out actions like appending results for 620 and 621 to the CSV and submitting Idea 7 with appropriate settings while ensuring code remains efficient."
