  \ The management tool only shows ticks up to 350 right now.**Planning token management actions**\n\nI can still go to token management to prune blocks 343, 349, 347, and 342 since 342 has a high count of 4125.\n\nI’ll craft precise prune summaries with evaluation IDs and results. Then I’ll log the GRU head results to a storage CSV and notes, updating for both negative results. We might want to avoid spending tokens.\n\nFor the next research steps, I’ll write a second archive addendum summarizing dynamic MoT results. The user hasn't specified a question yet, so I’ll focus on executing actions like updating storage and pruning tokens.**Clarifying scoring metrics**\n\nFor score 617, I see that -0.026306 improved slightly by 8.1e-5. My understanding is that the scores reflect mean negative MAE, so a more negative score is actually better. \n\nComparing -0.026306 to the baseline of -0.026387, it seems worse because -0.026306 is less negative, but then if I look at the absolute values, -0.026306\
  \ is better since its MAE is smaller. \n\nSo, it looks like closer scores to zero are preferred based on leaderboard standards, and the highest performing scores are around -0.026125.**Clarifying MAE and planning actions**\n\nI need to remember that in negative MAE, a more negative score indicates a larger MAE. So, a better MAE yields a less negative value, meaning scores closer to zero are preferable. For example, -0.026306 is better than -0.026387, indicating a positive improvement from the baseline.\n\nNow, I must record GRU head results as negative and propose closing the GRU line. My next steps include pruning specific blocks and updating the storage with the latest CSV results while keeping everything straightforward without heavy formatting."
